namespace Jazz2 {
//###==##====#=====--==~--~=~- --- -- -  -  -   -

/** @page editor Level editor
@brief The level editor built into the game, and the formats it reads and writes

@tableofcontents

@section editor-overview Overview

The game has a level editor of its own, compiled in with the `WITH_EDITOR` option @m_span{m-text m-dim} (see
@ref building-config-params-game) @m_endspan. It's on by default everywhere except the platforms that can't save
anything --- the <b>Nintendo 64</b>, the <b>Dreamcast</b>, the <b>GameCube</b> and the <b>PlayStation 2</b> --- and
it isn't part of a dedicated server or of the Shareware Demo.

The editor works only with the game's own level format, never with the original @cpp ".j2l" @ce files. The
original levels are converted into that format on the first start @m_span{m-text m-dim} (see @ref paths) @m_endspan,
and can be opened from @cpp "Cache/" @ce like any other level.

@section editor-opening Opening the editor

-   <b>Level Editor</b> in the main menu opens a new, empty level with the dialog to open an existing one over it.
-   <b>Edit This Level</b> in the pause menu of a level played alone leaves the level and opens it in the editor.
-   The `/edit` command-line switch opens the editor directly, followed optionally by the level to open --- either
    `episode/level` as the game names it, or the path of a level file:

@code{.sh}
Jazz2 /edit prince/01_castle1
Jazz2 /edit "C:\Levels\MyLevel.j2l"
@endcode

The level is shown at the size of the window, behind the panels of the editor, and the level music plays muffled as
it does under the pause menu. The layers move at their own speeds as the camera moves, placed as the game would place
them for a player at the center of the view --- <b>Flat Layers</b> in the <b>View</b> menu puts all of them in the
same grid instead, which makes the background layers easier to edit. A layer that moves by itself, by its automatic
speed, stays still while it's edited, and <b>Pause Layer Movement</b> (<kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>Shift</kbd>+<kbd>P</kbd>)
keeps all of them still, where their offsets put them.

@section editor-interface The interface

-   The <b>menu bar</b> and the <b>toolbar</b> at the top.
-   The <b>tools</b> on the left, with the stamp the brush paints with below them, and the <b>tiles</b> and the
    <b>events</b> to pick from next to them.
-   The <b>layers</b> on the right, with the properties of the layer being edited below them. The eye hides a layer
    in the editor and the lock keeps it from being changed --- neither changes the level itself. A layer is moved in
    the list by dragging it and renamed by a double click on it. A layer without a name is shown by what it is, dimmed
    --- a textured background, effects, a masked layer, a foreground or a background --- and the names are saved
    with the level only for the editor, an exported level doesn't have them. <b>Resize Layer</b> in the <b>Layer</b>
    menu changes its size, with the point it stays fixed at, and fills where it grows with empty tiles, with its edge
    tiles going on, or with the layer repeated. Events and the water level move along with the sprite layer. In the
    view, outside of the layer being edited is darkened where it doesn't repeat; a layer that repeats has a frame
    around where its tiles really are and fainter lines where it starts again. Besides its speed
    and offset, a layer has a <b>model</b> for each axis --- moving by its speed, fixed to the screen, fitted to the
    level or moving by the size of the view --- and a <b>renderer</b>: tiles, a solid color, tinted, x-ray, blur
    behind, or the sky and circle backgrounds. <b>Movement</b> sets the speeds and models of a common kind of layer
    at once --- a foreground, the layers of the background further and further away, a layer fixed to the screen or
    fitted to the level --- and shows <em>Custom</em> for anything else. What the <b>color</b> of a layer means
    depends on the renderer, so the inspector shows it as that renderer takes it: a color the tiles are multiplied
    by, the <b>tint</b> of a tinted layer --- an entry of the palette, picked from it by clicking the swatch, and its
    strength --- or the <b>horizon</b> and the <b>stars</b> of a sky or a circle. A sky or a circle is drawn as the
    plain tiles of its layer while the layer is edited, the picture made of them couldn't be painted on.
-   Every color written as red, green and blue --- the color and the horizon of a layer, the ambient color of the
    level, an entry of its palette --- has a swatch next to it that opens a <b>color picker</b>
    (@ref Editor::UiColorPicker): saturation and value in a square, hue in a bar next to it, and alpha in another
    one where the color has it. The color changes as it's dragged, as one step that can be undone; the swatch of the
    color before brings it back, and `#RRGGBB` can be written too.
-   The <b>Level</b> menu opens the <b>properties</b> of the level --- its name, its author, the next, secret and
    bonus levels, the music, the light at the start, the weather, the water level, the tile shown with it in the list of levels,
    and the pit and multiplayer flags --- and its <b>texts</b> (<kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>T</kbd>). The folder next to a
    level picks its file, a level of the same episode is written without the episode as the original levels have it.
    A new level has the player name of the options as its author, <b>Me</b> puts it there again; the list of custom
    levels shows the author after the name. The one next to the music picks it from the `Music` directories of the user content and `Content`, or from the
    original game, and the music starts playing right away. The <b>caption tile</b> is a tile of the default tile
    set whose colors light up an RGB keyboard while the level is played, the brush next to it takes the tile of the
    brush. Events refer to a text
    by its number, so texts are only added and removed at the end. A text has as many lines as it needs, <kbd>Enter</kbd>
    breaks a line and <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>Enter</kbd> confirms it. Formatting is shown as it's written, as `\f`, and
    a backslash itself as two of them.
-   <b>Animated Tiles</b> in the <b>Level</b> menu lists the animated tiles of the level on the left, with what is
    selected of them on the right. A new one is made of the tiles of the brush, <b>Add Brush</b> adds them as frames
    after the selected frame --- the window stays open while tiles are picked. The time of a frame is in frames of the game, at 60 a second, the delay keeps
    the last frame longer, and an animation can play forward and back. A removed animated tile is replaced by its
    first frame wherever the level shows it. <b>Paint with It</b> makes it the brush.
-   <b>Tile Sets</b> in the <b>Level</b> menu changes the default tile set and the <b>extra tile sets</b>, whose tiles
    follow those of the default one. An extra tile set takes a range of the tiles of its file, and its colors can be
    matched to the palette of the level, each to the one that looks the closest --- otherwise its palette indices are
    used as they are. The tiles of the level follow every change: a tile shows the same picture after a tile set
    before it is removed or moved, or after its range starts elsewhere, and tiles of a removed tile set or past the
    end of a range are cleared, all as one step that can be undone. The tile IDs of the level grow as needed, more
    than 4096 of them can't all be used by scripts.
-   A double click on a tile of the tile picker, or <b>Edit Tile of the Brush</b> in the <b>Level</b> menu, changes
    the <b>pixels</b> and the collision <b>mask</b> of the tile, only in this level --- its tile set stays as it is.
    The pencil, the line, the rectangle (filled with <kbd>Shift</kbd>) and the fill paint with the left button and
    erase with the right one, <b>Replace</b> gives every pixel of the color clicked another one, the selection is dragged
    to move its pixels and the right button clears them, the eyedropper picks a color. The buttons below flip, rotate
    and shift the whole tile --- pixels and mask together, so the collision stays where the picture is, and what is
    shifted out comes back on the other side. The edges of the tiles around it in its tile set are shown dimmed around
    it. <b>Paint in Level</b> paints the tile right in the level instead: every place the layer being edited shows it is
    outlined, and a stroke over any of them paints the pixel under the pointer, flipped as the tile is, while all of
    them change together (@ref Editor::TilePixelTool). For the mask, <b>Mask from Pixels</b> makes solid every pixel
    that isn't transparent, <b>Fill Below Surface</b> makes solid everything below a surface drawn with the pencil,
    <b>Slope</b> makes a straight surface between the heights given for the left and the right edge, <b>Mask of Brush
    Tile</b> takes the mask of another tile, <b>Empty</b> and <b>Solid</b> clear or fill it. <b>As in Tile Set</b> drops
    the changes.
    The level shows the tile as it's painted. The level keeps palette indices, so a true color tile is reduced to
    the closest colors of the palette. <b>Export PNG</b> and <b>Import PNG</b> take the tile to another program and
    back --- an image in the palette of the level keeps its indices, other colors become the closest entries, and
    only the top left 32 × 32 pixels are taken.
-   A tile of the <b>tiles of this level</b> is changed right in the level instead, and a true color one in true color:
    the swatch opens a color picker with how see-through the color is, the palette gives its colors too, and an image
    is imported with its colors as they are. <b>Copy into This Level</b> in the window of any other tile copies it into
    the tiles of this level, as the level shows it, and the level then shows the copy wherever it showed the tile, all
    as one step --- so any tile can be changed in true color, only in this level, which the original game's 8-bit
    overrides can't.
-   <b>New Tile Set</b> in the <b>Level</b> menu makes a tile set of empty tiles in the palette of the level, saved
    into the user content as `Tilesets/<name>.j2t` and added to the level as an extra tile set or its default one.
    Its tiles are painted as tiles changed in the level, and saved into it as below.
-   <b>Add Tiles from Image</b> in the <b>Level</b> menu adds the tiles of an image, cut as for a new tile set, to the
    end of a tile set of the level, in the closest colors of its palette. Tiles the tile set has already can be
    skipped. The tile set is saved into the user content --- one that isn't there yet is copied there, and the copy is
    used instead of it from then on. Its tiles that were there keep their places, and so does every tile of the level.
    <b>Add to</b> can also make a <b>New tile set</b> file of the tiles right there, which the level then uses as an
    extra tile set, or put them into the <b>Tiles of this level</b>.
-   The <b>tiles of this level</b> are an extra tile set the level stores in itself, they aren't in any tile set file
    and no other level has them (@ref Formats::ExtraTileSetRecord::Embedded). It's made when tiles are first added to
    it, it's then listed with the other extra tile sets, and its tiles are painted, changed and saved into it as those
    of any other. Undo takes added tiles away again. Picking another tile set in its place drops the tiles; saving
    the tiles changed in the level into a new tile set moves them into a file.
-   <b>Save Tile Changes into Tile Set</b> in the <b>Level</b> menu, or <b>Save into Tile Set</b> in the window of a
    tile, makes the tiles changed in this level tiles of a tile set, and the level doesn't change them anymore. They go
    into a new tile set the level then uses instead, saved into the user content as `Tilesets/<name>.j2t`, or into the
    tile set they're from --- every level that uses it shows them then. A tile set that isn't in the user content
    already is copied there, and the copy is used instead of it from then on. A true color tile stays one, in the
    colors the level showed; the tiles of an extra tile set remapped to the palette of the level get the indices of
    their own file back. Undo puts the changes back into the level, the saved tile set stays as it is.
-   <b>Rearrange Tile Set</b> in the <b>Level</b> menu deletes tiles of the default tile set, moves them to other
    places and puts empty tiles between them (@ref Editor::ShowTileSetLayoutDialog()). Every level that uses the tile set
    refers to its tiles by their places, so the levels that use it are found first: the tile set can be saved as itself
    --- a copy in the user content the whole game uses then --- with every level in a place that can be written to
    changed to the new places of the tiles, while those in the files of the game are listed, as they'd show other
    tiles; or as a new tile set only this level uses then, which leaves the others as they are. Tiles of the level that
    were deleted are cleared. The files changed can't be undone, the level itself can.
-   <b>Export Tile Set Image</b> in the <b>Level</b> menu saves the default tile set as an image of its tiles, in rows
    of 10 as the original tile sets were drawn, and another one of their masks next to it, `.mask.png`. A tile set of
    8-bit tiles is saved indexed with its own palette, entry 0 transparent, so nothing is lost. The editor reads and
    writes PNG on its own (@ref Editor::PngCodec), all kinds of PNG images are read.
-   <b>New Tile Set from Image</b> in the <b>Level</b> menu makes a new tile set of an image, cut into tiles of
    32 × 32 pixels row by row, and saves it into the user content as `Tilesets/<name>.j2t` --- a name of the game's
    tile sets or of another one that exists isn't taken. An indexed image can keep its own palette, any image can
    keep its colors as true color, take the closest colors of the palette of the level, or get <b>a palette made for
    it</b>. That one keeps the entries the sprites use, so they look as they did --- a tile set often changes their
    colors a little, so an entry close to the color of the sprites counts too --- and the other entries are given the
    colors that make the image look closest (@ref Editor::BuildPalette(), a median cut refined by k-means, compared as
    they look). With <b>Dither</b>, colors between two entries are mixed by an ordered pattern that continues from one
    tile to the next. <b>Skip tiles that are the same as another</b> leaves out repeated tiles, empty ones too. The
    image of masks next to
    it (`.mask.png`) gives the masks, otherwise the tiles are solid where they aren't transparent. The first tile is
    always the empty one, an empty tile is put first if the image doesn't start with one, and empty tiles at its end
    are left out. The new tile set can be added to the level as an extra tile set, or become its default one. A tile
    set exported by <b>Export Tile Set Image</b>, changed in another program, comes back the same this way.
-   <b>Palette</b> in the <b>Level</b> menu shows the palette the level is in --- that of its default tile set,
    unless the level has its own. Its own starts as a copy of the one it's in, and only its own can be changed. An
    entry is picked by a click, a range of them by dragging or with <kbd>Shift</kbd>, and the first one picked is
    edited as red, green and blue, as hue, saturation and value, as `#RRGGBB`, or with the color picker of its
    swatch. <b>Gradient</b> fills the range with
    even steps between the colors at its ends, as they look, <b>From Tile Set</b> gives it the colors of the tile set
    back. The window stays open and the level is seen in the colors as they change, the sprites too, as the game shows
    them. <b>Into Tile Set</b> makes the palette of the level the palette of its default tile set, or of a new tile set
    the level then uses (see <b>Save Tile Changes into Tile Set</b> below), and the level doesn't have its own then.
    <b>Import</b> takes the colors of a palette of another program --- JASC (.pal), GIMP (.gpl) or Adobe (.act)
    --- or of an image (.png): the palette of an indexed one, or a grid of 16 × 16 colors. They go into the own
    palette of the level, the first entry stays transparent. A palette exported as an image is such a grid, indexed. <b>Export</b> saves the palette the level
    is shown in, in the format the extension of its name gives (@ref Editor::PaletteFile). Below the color, the
    window tells how many pixels of the 8-bit tiles the level shows use the entry; <b>Unused</b> crosses out the entries
    no tile uses --- sprites may still use some of them --- and <b>Next</b> picks the next of them. The arrows move the
    picked entries an entry earlier or later and <b>Swap</b> swaps the first and the last picked one. With <b>Keep the
    look of the level</b> the pixels go with their colors, so the level looks the same --- the tiles of tile sets are
    changed in this level for it, the tiles of this level right in it --- otherwise the pixels show the colors now in
    their places. Either is one step.
-   The <b>tiles</b> of the tile sets of the level, next to the tools. A click picks a tile, a drag picks a rectangle
    of them. The arrows next to the name switch between the tile sets and the animated tiles, <kbd>Ctrl</kbd> with the
    wheel or the buttons on the right change how many tiles there are in a row, 10 at first --- the original tile
    sets are drawn in rows of 10, so their larger pieces are whole then. With the brush, the row <b>Next</b> above them
    suggests the tiles the levels have next to the tiles around the cell under the pointer, a click makes one the brush;
    <b>Continue Row by Suggestions</b> (<kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>Shift</kbd>+<kbd>Right</kbd>) fills the empty cells right of
    the pointer with them, the one the levels have there the most each. The <b>Events</b> tab next to <b>Tiles</b> shows
    the events instead, see @ref editor-events.
-   The <b>status bar</b> at the very bottom tells how the tool in use works --- or what the buttons of a gamepad do,
    while one is used --- which layer is edited, which tile and event are under the pointer and how close the view is.

The panel with the tiles and the events and the one on the right are resized by dragging their inner edges, the list
of layers by dragging the edge between it and the properties below it, and they keep their size. Both panels can
also float over the level (@ref Editor::UiDockFrame): the button at the end of the header of a panel makes it
float, or drag the header by its dotted grip --- it comes off once it was dragged a few units. A floating panel is
moved by its header and resized by its edges or the dotted corner, the one used last is in front. Dragged back to
the edge it came from, the place it docks at lights up, and let go there it docks again; the button in its header
or a double click on the header does the same. The panel glides between its two places while the room it takes when
docked opens or closes. Whether each panel floats and where is kept in `Jazz2.Editor.json`.

On a small screen the interface is laid out for it: narrower than 600 units, the two panels become drawers that take
no room --- the buttons at the start of the toolbar slide them over the view and back, one at a time, and the cross in
the header closes one; narrower than 420 units, the tools are a row at the bottom and the toolbar keeps only what the
menus don't have. <b>View</b> chooses the layout by the size of the window, or always the one for a desktop or the
compact one. <b>Expert Mode</b> in the <b>View</b> menu, on at first, offers all tools and commands; without it, only
the simple ones are in the tools and the menus. The interface is made larger or smaller in steps of a quarter up to 3
times its size, then larger ones.

Dialogs are moved by dragging their title, a window clicked into comes in front of the other windows. The cross
next to the title cancels a dialog as Escape does, by its last button. Enter chooses the accented button. The
selection is outlined by short marching dashes, as a bitmap editor has it.
The interface is scaled by the size of the window. Its text is drawn a little smaller than the rest, at
@ref Editor::UiTheme::TextScale of the scale. Labels of properties take as much room as the longest of them needs,
a label too long for that is put above its value.

@section editor-controls Controls

@subsection editor-controls-mouse Mouse

-   The <b>left button</b> uses the tool, the <b>right button</b> picks the tile under the pointer with the brush and
    erases with the rectangle and the fill.
-   The <b>wheel</b> zooms at the pointer. Zooming goes through steps at which every pixel of the level is just as large.
    With <kbd>Shift</kbd> it moves the view sideways, with <kbd>Ctrl</kbd> up and down, with <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>Shift</kbd>
    it makes the interface larger or smaller and with <kbd>Alt</kbd> it changes the layer being edited. A wheel that
    turns sideways moves the view sideways. The view zooms out until a tile is a pixel; once a tile is smaller than
    about 6 pixels of the screen, the layers are drawn as images of 4 pixels a tile, made from 64 × 64 tiles at a time and again when their
    tiles change (@ref Editor::TileLod), so even a large level is drawn quickly as a whole.
-   The <b>middle button</b>, or the left one with <kbd>Space</kbd> held, moves the view. The view keeps moving for a while
    after it's let go.
-   A number can be dragged, typed after a click, or changed by the arrows at its right end --- a click on them or the
    wheel over them, by ten with <kbd>Shift</kbd>.

@subsection editor-controls-gamepad Gamepad

As soon as a button of a gamepad is pressed, a cursor of its own is shown and the status bar tells what the buttons do.
The mouse or a key takes over again.

Gamepad | Action
------- | ------
Left stick | Move the cursor, faster the farther it's pushed and the longer it's held
Directional pad | Move the cursor by a tile of the layer being edited, the view follows --- over a number, change it
Right stick | Move the view, faster the longer it's held --- over a list, scroll it
Left / right trigger | Zoom out / in at the cursor, smoothly, faster the farther it's pulled
A | Use the tool, or what the cursor is over in the interface
B | Erase, or close what is shown over the interface
X | Pick the tile or the event under the cursor
Y | Next tool; held, the tools in a ring around the cursor, the left stick points at one and letting go of Y picks it --- over a text or a number, type into it with a keyboard on the screen
Left / right bumper | Edit the previous / next layer
Back / left stick press | Undo / redo
Start | Play

A text is typed with the keyboard of the system where it has one, otherwise with the editor's own at the bottom of the
screen (@ref Editor::UiOnScreenKeyboard), used by the cursor as any button.

@subsection editor-controls-touch Touch screen

On a touch screen the status bar tells what the fingers do (@ref Editor::TouchGestures).

Fingers | Action
------- | ------
One | Use the tool --- it starts once the finger moved a little, so a second finger soon after doesn't paint; a quick tap is a click. On the interface, a finger is a pointer
Two | Move the view and zoom it by pinching
Tap with two / three | Undo / redo
Hold one still | The tools in a ring around it, a tap picks one
Tap on a box to type into | The keyboard of the system, or the editor's own

@subsection editor-controls-keyboard Keyboard

Keys | Action
---- | ------
<kbd>B</kbd>, <kbd>E</kbd>, <kbd>R</kbd>, <kbd>G</kbd>, <kbd>I</kbd> | Brush, eraser, rectangle, fill, eyedropper
<kbd>M</kbd>, <kbd>W</kbd>, <kbd>Q</kbd>, <kbd>H</kbd> | Select, magic wand, events, hand
<kbd>Space</kbd> / <kbd>Alt</kbd> (held) | Hand / eyedropper for a while
<kbd>X</kbd> / <kbd>Y</kbd> | Flip the stamp horizontally / vertically
<kbd>Z</kbd> / <kbd>Shift</kbd>+<kbd>Z</kbd> | Paint a tile of the stamp at random in each cell, for the brush, the rectangle and the fill / choose other tiles
<kbd>1</kbd> -- <kbd>9</kbd> | Edit a layer, with <kbd>Shift</kbd> hide or show it
<kbd>Page Up</kbd> / <kbd>Page Down</kbd> | Edit the previous / next layer
<kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>Z</kbd> / <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>Y</kbd> | Undo / redo
<kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>X</kbd>, <kbd>C</kbd>, <kbd>V</kbd> | Cut, copy, paste --- pasted tiles become the stamp of the brush
<kbd>Del</kbd> | Delete the selected tiles
<kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>A</kbd> / <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>D</kbd> | Select all / deselect
<kbd>+</kbd>, <kbd>-</kbd> | Zoom in, out
<kbd>0</kbd> / <kbd>Home</kbd> | Zoom as in the game / show the whole level
Arrows | Move the view
<kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>G</kbd> / <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>P</kbd> | Show the grid / flat layers
<kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>Shift</kbd>+<kbd>P</kbd> | Keep the layers that move by themselves still
<kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>Shift</kbd>+<kbd>E</kbd> | Show the events while other tools are used
<kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>M</kbd> | Show the collision masks of the layer being edited over its tiles, then instead of them, then not at all --- darkened, with a smooth outline around whole shapes. On the sprite layer, a symbol shows what an event makes a tile do instead of its mark: one-way, vine, hook, poles, hurting sides, instant death, destructible by weapons, by buttstomp or by speed, collapsing, ricochet, slide, no climbing, tube and trigger
<kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>T</kbd> | Edit the texts of the level
<kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>N</kbd>, <kbd>O</kbd>, <kbd>S</kbd> | New, open, save
<kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>Shift</kbd>+<kbd>S</kbd> / <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>E</kbd> | Save as / export
<kbd>F5</kbd>, <kbd>Shift</kbd>+<kbd>F5</kbd>, <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>F5</kbd> | Play, play here, play from start --- <kbd>F5</kbd> again returns
<kbd>F6</kbd> / <kbd>Alt</kbd>+<kbd>F5</kbd> | Put the play-start marker at the pointer / play from it
<kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>Shift</kbd>+<kbd>Right</kbd> | Continue the row right of the pointer by the tiles the levels have there
<kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>L</kbd> | Show the path of the last play-test
<kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>Shift</kbd>+<kbd>+</kbd>, <kbd>-</kbd>, <kbd>0</kbd> | Larger, smaller interface, by the size of the window
<kbd>Esc</kbd> | Cancel what the tool is doing, then deselect
<kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>K</kbd> | Command palette --- search everything the editor can do by its name, <kbd>Enter</kbd> does the first one found
<kbd>F1</kbd> | Keyboard shortcuts, each can be changed there
<kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>H</kbd> | History --- a click on a change goes back or forward to it
<kbd>F2</kbd> | Navigator --- the whole level small, a click or a drag moves the view there, with the bookmarks and the notes
<kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>Shift</kbd>+<kbd>1</kbd> -- <kbd>9</kbd> / <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>1</kbd> -- <kbd>9</kbd> | Set a bookmark in the middle of the view / go to it
<kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>Shift</kbd>+<kbd>N</kbd> | Add a note where the pointer is, it's written in the navigator
<kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>F</kbd> | Find and replace tiles or events
<kbd>U</kbd> | Measure --- a drag measures in pixels and tiles and shows how far the player jumps from its start
<kbd>K</kbd> | Events along a path --- clicks make a path, the right button places the event to place along it

A shortcut is changed in <b>Keyboard Shortcuts</b> by a click on it and the keys of the new one, <kbd>Backspace</kbd>
removes it, <kbd>Esc</kbd> keeps it. Another action that had the same shortcut is left without one. Changed shortcuts
are saved in @cpp "Jazz2.EditorHotkeys.json" @ce in the configuration directory, as an object of identifiers of the
actions, each with @cpp [key, modifiers] @ce or @cpp null @ce for none (@ref Editor::ActionRegistry::LoadOverrides());
<b>Reset All</b> brings back all the defaults.

Bookmarks and notes are only for the editor, they're kept with the level in the @cpp "anno" @ce chunk (see
@ref editor-formats-chunks) and an exported level has none of them. They're not undone, but the level is unsaved
after they change (@ref Editor::Annotation).

Copied tiles go to the clipboard of the system too, as a short text starting with @cpp {"J2R": @ce, so they can be
pasted into another level, also in another instance of the game.

@section editor-events Events

Everything that isn't a tile --- where the level starts, enemies, things to collect, springs, warps, what a tile does
when the player touches it --- is an event. Events are placed on the tiles of the sprite layer, one event on a tile,
whichever layer is being edited.

The <b>Events</b> tab next to the tools lists them by category, with a search box. A click on one chooses it and the
<b>event tool</b> (<kbd>Q</kbd>). Events the game doesn't use are found only by searching.

-   A click on an empty tile places the event, dragging goes on placing it on the tiles passed. With
    <kbd>Shift</kbd> it's placed over events already there.
-   A click on an event selects it, <kbd>Ctrl</kbd> adds it to the selection or takes it out of it. A drag over empty
    tiles with <kbd>Ctrl</kbd> selects the events in a rectangle. Dragging selected events moves them.
-   The <b>right button</b> erases events, <kbd>Alt</kbd> with a click picks the event under the pointer to place it
    elsewhere with all its parameters.
-   <kbd>Del</kbd>, <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>C</kbd>, <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>X</kbd> and <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>V</kbd> delete,
    copy, cut and paste the selected events while the event tool is used. Pasted events are placed by a click, as
    they were placed to each other. Copied events go to the clipboard of the system too, as tiles do. A warp copied
    together with its target, or a trigger together with the tiles it changes, gets an identifier the level doesn't
    use yet when it's pasted, so the copy works on its own. A part of a warp or a trigger keeps its identifier and
    joins the one in the level.

While the event tool is used, the panel on the right shows the parameters of the selected events --- or of the event
to place, when none is selected. A change applies to all selected events of the type of the first one, the
difficulties and the other flags to all of them. The raw parameters at the bottom show all 16 bytes of the event,
for what the parameters above don't cover.

In the level, an event is drawn as the object it makes looks with its parameters, with a small mark of the color of
its category in the corner of its tile. Events that aren't objects are drawn as boxes of that color with their name.
The name gets smaller with the box, and when it would get too small, it's broken into two lines, or only its initials
are left. The badges tell what isn't seen otherwise:

-   white and dark squares at the bottom right --- in which of the easy, normal and hard difficulties the event is,
    when it isn't in all of them,
-   a blue square at the top right --- the object appears again after it's gone,
-   a purple square at the bottom left --- the event is there only in multiplayer.

Warps are joined to their targets by blue arrows, a warp without a target is crossed out in red. A selected event with
a trigger shows the events of the same trigger. A selected light shows how far it reaches, a change of the water
level shows the new level, and a camera limit shows where the camera is kept, darkening the rest.

A single selected event has <b>handles</b> --- small white squares --- for the parameters that put something in the
level: the radii of a light, the width of a bridge, the length of the chain of a spike ball or a moving platform, where
a pole stands, the new water level, and the left and right edges of a camera limit. Dragging a handle changes the
parameter as one step that can be undone, the level shows it as it changes (@ref Editor::EventGizmos). Dragging the
left edge of a camera limit keeps its right edge where it is, and its right edge dragged to the end of the level reaches
there whatever the width of the level becomes.

<b>Select Events of the Same Kind</b> in the <b>Edit</b> menu selects every event of the kind of the first selected one.
<b>Copy Event to Every Tile Like Its Own</b> places the selected event on every tile of the sprite layer that shows the
same tile, flipped the same way, and has no event yet --- a hook or a one-way platform is placed once and then copied
to all of its tiles --- as one step that can be undone.

<b>Statistics</b> in the <b>Level</b> menu counts what the level has in each difficulty: enemies, bosses, gems and coins
by their value (those in crates and barrels too), food, carrots, extra lives, ammo, containers, checkpoints, warps and
exits, generators and the starts of each character --- a character without a start is shown in red. Events only in
multiplayer aren't counted (@ref Editor::LevelStatistics).

<b>Find and Replace</b> (<kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>F</kbd>) finds a tile, taken from the brush, flipped the same way or
any way, in the layer being edited or in all of them; <b>Select</b> selects it in the layer being edited, <b>Replace</b>
puts another tile from the brush in its place, flipped and translucent as it was. Events are found by their kind,
chosen in the list or taken from the selection, and replaced by another kind with its default parameters, keeping their
difficulties and flags. Each replacement is one step that can be undone.

The <b>Measure</b> tool (<kbd>U</kbd>) measures a drag in pixels and tiles, with <kbd>Ctrl</kbd> from the middle of
a tile to the middle of another, and draws the arcs of a walking jump, a dashing jump and the double jump of Spaz from
where it starts, as the original game jumps (the `Legacy` constants of @ref Actors::Player), telling which of them gets
over to where it ends --- walls and ceilings on the way aren't taken into account.

The <b>Events Along Path</b> tool (<kbd>K</kbd>) places the event to place at even distances along a path, as a row or
an arc of coins or gems: clicks add the points, with <kbd>Ctrl</kbd> in the middle of a tile, the events are shown where
they'll be and the right button places them as one step. A window next to the view sets the distance between them,
whether the path is a smooth curve through the points, and whether they're placed off the grid, exactly on the path,
or on the tiles it goes through.

The <b>Smart Brush</b> (<kbd>T</kbd>) paints ground and chooses its tiles by themselves: the left button makes cells
solid, the right one empty, and every painted cell and those around it get the tile the levels of the tile set use the
most where the same of the 8 cells around are solid, the one that goes best with the tiles next to it --- so a painted
block gets its top, edges and corners, and painting next to ground joins them. Nothing has to be set up for it: when it's
first used, every level that has the tile set as its default one is read, a few a frame, and the status bar tells how
far it is (@ref Editor::SmartTileModel). What was learned is kept in @cpp "Cache/Editor/Adjacency/" @ce, so the next
time the tile set is known right away, until a level that uses it changes. Tiles around of another tile set, animated
ones, and those the levels use with their neighbors as they are now stay as they are. <b>Choose Tiles by Their Neighbors</b> in the <b>Edit</b> menu chooses
the selected tiles again so.

<b>Fill Selection with Ground</b> in the <b>Edit</b> menu makes all selected cells ground, their tiles and those around
chosen as by the Smart Brush, as one step. <b>Beautify Selection</b> keeps the collision exactly as it is: each selected
tile can become another tile of the tile set with the same mask, flipped or not, the one that goes best with the tiles
around it --- each row is chosen as a whole, the best path through the tiles that can be in it, then each tile again
with all of its neighbors known. Tiles without collision are kept.

<b>Terrain Rules...</b> in the <b>Edit</b> menu shows the tile ground gets for each of the 47 ways the cells around it
can be solid --- a corner counts only when both sides next to it are solid, otherwise it can't be seen --- and lets it be
chosen by hand (@ref Editor::TerrainRules): a click gives a way the tiles of the brush, more of them are taken in turns by
the position of the cell, the right button returns it to the tile learned from the levels. Tiles chosen by hand are
framed, and the Smart Brush, the Ground Line tool and choosing tiles by their neighbors take them first. Tiles of extra
tile sets can be chosen too, they're found by the name of their tile set in any level that has it. A tile set can have
several <b>terrains</b> --- ground, a wall, a cloud --- each with rules of its own, the one chosen at the top is the one
the tools use. <b>Slopes</b> tags the tile of the brush as a slope with the heights of its surface at both edges, or
leaves it out of ground, for tiles whose masks don't tell it right; the Ground Line tool then takes the tags. <b>Try It</b>
is a small area where cells are made solid or empty by clicks, and get tiles as the tools would choose them. All of it
is kept in the rules of the tile set with its prefabs. The game comes with rules for the tile sets `castle1`, `diam1` and
`jungle2`, made from what their levels use the most and meant to be refined (`Content/Tilesets/*.rules`).

<b>Paint Tiles of Stamp at Random</b> (<kbd>Z</kbd>, in the <b>Edit</b> menu) makes the brush, the rectangle and the
fill put one of the tiles of the stamp in each cell, chosen by the position of the cell, so painting over it again
doesn't change it --- a tile there more times in the stamp comes more often, and a tile left out of the stamp leaves the
cell as it is, to scatter things. <b>Choose Other Tiles at Random</b> (<kbd>Shift</kbd>+<kbd>Z</kbd>) chooses again.

<b>Prefabs...</b> in the <b>Edit</b> menu keeps stamps under a name with the default tile set of the level, for any
level of it (@ref Editor::TilePrefab): <b>Save Brush</b> saves the brush as one, <b>Use</b> makes one the brush. Tiles
of extra tile sets are found by the name of their tile set, animated tiles are left out. They're kept in the rules of
the tile set, `Tilesets/<name>.rules`, a JSON file looked for as tile sets are and saved into the user content.

The <b>Ground Line</b> tool (<kbd>P</kbd>) lays ground along a line: clicks add points of the surface, with
<kbd>Ctrl</kbd> at a corner of a tile, and the right button lays it as one step. The tiles the line goes through get the
pieces of ground whose collision masks follow it the closest --- flat, or sloped as steeply as the line --- of those the
levels use with the same neighbors (@ref Editor::TileShapes); below them it's filled down to the ground that's there, at
most 6 tiles deep, and those tiles and the ones around are chosen as by the Smart Brush. The line goes from left to
right. The window of the tool chooses what is laid: <b>Ground</b> below the line, a <b>Ceiling</b> above it --- of tiles
whose masks are solid above a straight line, or of pieces of ground upside down --- a floating <b>Band</b> as thick as
the depth, or a closed <b>Shape</b> whose points go around it. <b>Smooth curve</b> makes the line a curve through its
points. The options also change the line clicked last.

The line is kept with the level (@ref Editor::TerrainSpline, the editor-only chunk @cpp "spln" @ce), drawn with its
points while the tool is used: a point can be dragged --- the ground follows while it's dragged, and is laid when it's
let go --- a click on the line adds a point there to drag, <kbd>Shift</kbd>+click removes one, and the right button on
the line removes its ground. Where the collision of the ground laid is, its surface is drawn over the line, green where
it follows the line and red where it misses it. <b>Make Tiles Along Ground Line</b> in the <b>Edit</b> menu cuts the
solid tile the levels use the most along the line clicked last, into tiles of this level, so the collision follows
the line exactly --- also with a tile set without any slopes. Each change lays the ground again as one step:
the tiles it wrote that are still as it wrote them get back what was there before, and tiles changed by hand since stay
as they are and aren't written by it anymore. <b>Make Ground Line from Selection</b> in the <b>Edit</b> menu makes a line
along the top of the selected ground of the level, whose tiles are then its own with nothing before them --- so ground
of any level can be reshaped by its line, and dragging it lower takes ground away.

The <b>tile picker</b> shows how often the level uses each tile with the magnifier button in its header: unused tiles
are dark, used ones have a bar as long as how often, the tooltip tells the number.

<b>Generate Effect Tiles</b> in the <b>Level</b> menu makes tiles for a layer drawn as Blur Behind --- clear glass,
glass blocks, frosted glass, water, heat haze, a magnifier or a puddle --- of a size in tiles and a strength, adds them
to the end of the tiles of this level by default, or of a tile set of the level or a new one, and makes them the brush
(@ref Editor::GenerateFxTiles()).

When the level is saved, a small image of where it starts is kept with it (the @cpp "PRVW" @ce chunk, see
@ref Formats::LevelFile::PreviewChunk): what the game shows around the start of Jazz, all layers at the parallax of
the game, a third of its size. The list of custom levels shows it next to the name of the selected level, with a soft
shadow, once the level stays selected for a moment. <b>Auto</b> next to the caption tile in the properties of the level
picks a colorful tile the level uses a lot.

<b>Show Where the Player Can Get</b> in the <b>View</b> menu or the toolbar (<kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>Shift</kbd>+<kbd>R</kbd>) loads
the level again and tints green every tile the player can get to from the start of the character it's played with,
brighter nearer the start. It uses the route tracer of the multiplayer minimap (@ref Multiplayer::ComputeReachableTiles()),
which walks, jumps, swims, climbs and takes springs, poles, tubes and warps as the original game does, but it's careful
rather than complete, so a way it doesn't know about can be missing. It's not updated as the level changes, it's turned
on again for that. <b>Check Level</b> uses it too: it tells when no exit can be reached from the start, and how many
things to collect may be out of reach.

<b>Check Level</b> in the <b>Level</b> menu looks for what's probably wrong (@ref Editor::CheckLevel()): Jazz without
a start (a character without a start starts where Jazz does), a level for multiplayer without a multiplayer start,
warps without a target, coin warps needing more coins than the level has, texts that are empty, triggers that are set
but change no tile and tiles of a trigger nothing sets, rolling rocks without a trigger and the other way around,
parameters out of their range, unknown events and more than one sky layer. Errors are red and come first, <b>Show</b>
moves the view to the event and selects it. The window stays open while the level is fixed, <b>Check Again</b> looks
again. A camera limit narrower than the screen of the game is widened to the left by the game, the editor shows where
the camera can go then, lighter, with a thinner edge.

<b>Off-grid events</b> --- objects at any position, not aligned to the tiles, as levels made in MLLE can have them
--- are kept, shown faded where they are, and played as the game plays them (@ref Events::EventMap): an object of
one is spawned at its own position, collected or destroyed for good, and spawned again after a checkpoint like any
other, a generator among them too. A start or a warp target among them keeps its exact position, a tile modifier is
applied to the tile it's in. With the event tool, <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>Shift</kbd>+click places the event to place
off the grid, exactly where it's clicked. A click on one selects it --- its parameters are then changed in the panel
on the right, <kbd>Delete</kbd> removes it and copying it copies it as an event to place on the grid --- dragging it
moves it, with <kbd>Shift</kbd> to the center of a tile, the right button erases it and <kbd>Alt</kbd> picks it as the
event to place. <b>Snap Off-Grid Events to Grid</b> in the <b>Level</b> menu moves them onto the tiles they're in, as
one step; one whose tile has an event already stays off the grid. Events are dimmed while another layer than the sprite layer is edited,
and hidden while other tools are used if <b>Show Events</b> (<kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>Shift</kbd>+<kbd>E</kbd>) is off.

@subsection editor-events-catalog Event catalog

What the editor knows about events is compiled in, in @ref Editor::EventCatalog. It's described as the game reads the
parameters --- in the code of the objects the events make, and the code that handles tiles and areas --- not as the
converter of the original levels writes them, because the converter reorders and scales them. Each event has a type,
a key that doesn't change between versions, a name and a description, a category, the parameters, and the sprite it's
shown with. The tables are split by categories into the files @cpp Editor/Events/EventCatalog.*.cpp @ce.

A parameter (@ref Editor::EventParamField) takes some bits of the 16 bytes of parameters, starting at a bit of a byte,
and says how it's edited --- a number, yes or no, one of a few options, flags whose options are masks, another
event, a text, the identifier of a warp, of a trigger or of a level text. Bytes no parameter takes are kept as they
are.

How an event looks depends on its parameters for many of them --- the kind of food, the color of a gem, which way a
spring points. @ref Editor::ResolveEventPreview() mirrors what the objects choose from their parameters, the catalog
gives the look of the others. A horizontal spring of no set orientation is turned away from the wall next to it, by the
tiles of the level, as the game turns it, a spring that launches up stands on the bottom of its tile, and a bridge is
shown as long as it is. The sprites are drawn in the level among its layers, as the objects of the game are, so the
layers in front of them cover them --- the marks of the events, their badges and the links between them stay over
everything. Links between events, such as a warp and its target, go from the edge of one mark to the edge of the other.

@subsection editor-events-extensions Events described from outside of the game

A script of a level can react to events the game doesn't know, or use those it knows in another way. It can describe
them for the editor by lines of its `.j2as` file, each one event in JSON, which the script itself ignores:

@code{.cpp}
#pragma editor-event {"type": 300, "key": "MyThing", "name": "My Thing", "category": "enemy", "preview": {"metadata": "Enemy/Turtle", "animation": 1}, "params": [{"name": "Speed", "type": "uint", "offset": 0, "bits": 4, "max": 15, "default": 3}, {"name": "Mode", "type": "enum", "offset": 0, "shift": 4, "bits": 2, "options": ["Walk", "Fly", "Swim"]}]}
@endcode

Event packs for all levels are `.json` files in `Editor/Events/` of the content or the user content, each one event,
an array of them, or an object with an array of them in `"events"`. The script of the level comes last, so its events
replace those of event packs, which replace those of the game. They're read when a level is opened or saved under
another name, and <b>Read Event Descriptions Again</b> in the <b>Level</b> menu reads them again after the script was
changed. What can be described is listed at @ref Editor::EventCatalog::LoadExtensions(); what's wrong is written into
the log and left out. An event no one describes is shown as its number, and only its raw parameters can be changed.

@section editor-playing Playing the level

The level can be played right from the editor, as it is at the moment --- saved or not. The view first flies to where
the game will show the player, as large as the game shows it, then the game is revealed in a ring growing from the
middle of the screen over the editor as it was, its panels too --- they go away once the ring has grown past them.
The ring has a soft, light color of the tiles of the sprite layer. A bar at the top to get back slides in then. The way
back reveals the editor in the same ring over the last frame of the game. The music goes on playing, it opens up over
a moment as the game is revealed and is muffled again on the way back.

-   <b>Play</b> (<kbd>F5</kbd>) continues the last play-test exactly where it was left, as long as the level wasn't
    changed since. When it was, the level starts again where the player was, with what the player had --- lives,
    health, score, weapons, gems, the shield --- and what was collected, destroyed or triggered stays so, except in
    the cells of the sprite layer and the events changed since, which start as new. A sprite layer of another size
    starts as new as a whole. Where memory is short (consoles with little of it), the play-test isn't kept, the next
    one only starts where the player was. Without a play-test to continue, the level starts at its start.
-   <b>Play Here</b> (<kbd>Shift</kbd>+<kbd>F5</kbd>) starts the level at the pointer, or at the center of the view.
    The player is placed at the nearest spot where it fits, and at the start of the level if there's none nearby.
-   <b>Play from Start</b> (<kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>F5</kbd>) starts the level at its start.
-   <b>Play from Marker</b> (<kbd>Alt</kbd>+<kbd>F5</kbd>) starts the level at the play-start marker, a green flag put
    by <b>Set Play Start Marker Here</b> (<kbd>F6</kbd>) at the pointer, kept with the level for the editor only, for
    trying the same part of a level again and again.

<b>Play Here</b> by its key starts at the pointer, chosen from the menu or by a button it starts in the middle of the
view.

The character and the difficulty to play with are chosen in the <b>Play</b> menu. <kbd>F5</kbd> or <b>Back to
Editor</b> in the pause menu returns to the editor, the view staying where the player was. So does leaving the level
by its exit, and a game over --- the next level is never loaded, and nothing is saved, neither the progress nor the
state of the level to resume later.

The <b>Play</b> menu also plays the level as <b>multiplayer in local splitscreen</b>, as the game does --- with the
rules of Cooperation, Battle, Team Battle, Race, Treasure Hunt or Capture the Flag and 1 to 4 players on one screen,
so starts for multiplayer, laps, bases and the rules can be tried (@ref Editor::PlayTestMpLevelHandler). <b>Alone</b>
plays as a single player again. The first player is the chosen character, the others take the next ones. Play Here
places all of them next to each other where it's asked to. A play-test kept to continue starts again when another game
mode or number of players is chosen.

The path the player took is drawn over the level after a play-test, smoothed, from blue at its start to gold at its
end, thin where the player was slow and thick where fast, with a red mark where the player died and a white one where the
play-test was left. <b>Show Play Path</b> (<kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>L</kbd>, also on the toolbar) hides it.

@section editor-files Files

-   <b>Save</b> writes the level back to its file. A level in @cpp "Cache/" @ce can't be saved there --- the cache is
    rebuilt from the original levels --- so it's saved as a new file instead.
-   <b>Save As</b> saves the level into the user content @m_span{m-text m-dim} (see @ref paths) @m_endspan, or into
    @cpp "Content/" @ce where it can be written to. A level of the game goes into the same episode there by default.
    When the same level exists in another place, the editor tells which of the two files the game will play ---
    the user content comes before @cpp "Content/" @ce, which comes before @cpp "Cache/" @ce. A script of the level
    (@cpp ".j2as" @ce) is copied along.
-   <b>Export</b> saves a level to be published, without the data only the editor uses. It checks the level first
    and tells about anything that would keep it from being played as intended, such as a missing start position.
    With <b>Don't allow editing</b> checked in the window to choose the file, the exported level gets @ref LevelFlags::NotEditable --- the game plays it as any
    other, but the editor doesn't open it, and the pause menu doesn't offer <b>Edit This Level</b>. Keep the level you
    edit to change it later. It only keeps the level from being opened by mistake, the flag is a bit of the file that
    anyone who wants to can clear.

Every file is written next to its target first and moved into place only when it's complete, so a failed save never
leaves a damaged level behind.

@subsection editor-files-recovery Recovery

While a level has unsaved changes, a copy of it is written every minute, and when the application is suspended,
into @cpp "Editor/Recovery/" @ce in the configuration directory. The copy is removed when the level is saved or its
changes are discarded. A copy still there when the level is opened again, newer than the level itself, is offered to
be restored.

@subsection editor-files-web In a browser

The web version has no files of its own computer to open: <b>From This Computer</b> in the window to open a level
uploads one into the game, and saving a level also downloads it. The settings and the recovery copies are kept in the
storage of the browser (@ref Editor::WebFileBridge).

@subsection editor-files-settings Settings

The settings of the editor are kept in @cpp "Jazz2.Editor.json" @ce in the configuration directory --- whether the
grid is shown, the size of the tiles to pick from, the scale of the interface (@cpp 0 @ce chooses it by the size of the
window), the layout (@cpp "Layout" @ce, @cpp 0 @ce by the size of the window, @cpp 1 @ce desktop, @cpp 2 @ce compact),
@cpp "Expert" @ce, where the panels float, the character, difficulty and players to play with, and the recently used
files, which the <b>File</b> menu lists. How a level was seen is kept with the level itself, see @cpp "edoc" @ce below.

@section editor-formats Level and tile set formats

Everything that reads or writes a level goes through @ref Formats::LevelReader and @ref Formats::LevelWriter, and a
tile set through @ref Formats::TileSetFile --- the converter of the original files, the game itself and the editor
alike. @ref Formats::LevelFile describes the layout of a level.

The editor holds a level as an @ref Editor::LevelDocument. Nothing is lost by loading a level into it and saving it
again, the file comes out byte for byte the same. Tiles are referred to there by their tile set and their index in
it, so adding a tile set or an animated tile doesn't change any layer.

@subsection editor-formats-chunks Chunks

A level can carry optional data at the end of its body, in chunks tagged by four characters. A reader skips the
chunks it doesn't know, so a level written by a newer version of the game still loads in an older one. A chunk whose
tag starts with a lowercase letter is editor-only data, such as how the editor was left or the splines the terrain
was made from --- the game never reads it, and exporting a level drops it.

Tag | What it holds
--- | -------------
@cpp "lnam" @ce | Names of the layers, one per layer in the order of the layers
@cpp "elay" @ce | State of the layers in the editor (hidden, locked), one byte per layer
@cpp "rcov" @ce | Path of the level file a recovery copy belongs to, only in recovery copies
@cpp "PRVW" @ce | Small image of the level for the list of custom levels, kept when the level is exported: @cpp u16 @ce width and height, then @cpp R, G, B @ce bytes row by row
@cpp "anno" @ce | Bookmarks and notes: @cpp varuint @ce count, then for each @cpp i32 @ce X and Y in pixels, @cpp u8 @ce number of the bookmark (0 for a note), @cpp varuint @ce length and the text
@cpp "edoc" @ce | How the level was seen and played in the editor (@ref Editor::DocumentViewState): @cpp u8 @ce version, @cpp u16 @ce flags (camera, grid, events, play path, flat layers, play settings, play-start marker), @cpp f32 @ce center X and Y and zoom relative to how large the game shows the level, @cpp i32 @ce layer, @cpp u8 @ce mask mode and picker panel, @cpp u8 @ce character, difficulty, game mode and players, @cpp i32 @ce X and Y of the play-start marker --- written whenever the level is saved, the level opens as it was left
@cpp "spln" @ce | Ground lines of the Ground Line tool: @cpp varuint @ce count, then for each @cpp i32 @ce layer, @cpp u8 @ce depth, @cpp u8 @ce kind (ground, ceiling, band, shape), @cpp u8 @ce flags (1 smooth), @cpp varuint @ce count of points and @cpp f32 @ce X and Y of each in pixels of the layer, @cpp varuint @ce count of tiles it wrote and for each @cpp i32 @ce X and Y and the tile written and the one before as @cpp u16 @ce index, @cpp u8 @ce source and @cpp u8 @ce flags

@subsection editor-formats-verify Verifying the formats

The `verify-formats` command of @ref asset-packer "AssetPacker" reads every level and tile set in a directory and
writes it back into memory, which has to give the same file byte for byte. A file written by another tool, which
compresses differently, has to read back to the same content instead.

@code{.sh}
AssetPacker verify-formats <directory>
@endcode

@section editor-testing Testing the editor

A debug build has a self-test of the editor. `/editor-selftest` loads every level in @cpp "Cache/Episodes/" @ce, or
in the directory given after it, into an @ref Editor::LevelDocument and checks that:

-   saving it again gives the same file
-   the data of the editor survives saving and loading, and exporting drops it
-   a random sequence of changes, undone, gives the original level back, and redone, the changed one

Before that, it checks the @ref editor-events-catalog "event catalog" --- that the parameters of an event don't
overlap, fit into their bits, and that a new event has the values they say. Then it goes through the events of all
the levels and lists in the log what the catalog doesn't expect: unknown events, values out of range or not among the
options, and bits no parameter takes. Most of what it lists are bytes the converter writes and the game ignores.

The result is written to the log, and the application quits.

@code{.sh}
Jazz2 /editor-selftest
@endcode

A debug build also runs the commands given by `/console` switches once the editor is open, one after another, so the
editor can be driven without input --- for example to take a screenshot of what a tool does. Positions are in pixels
of the window.

Command | What it does
------- | ------------
`/jump <x> <y> [scale]`, `/fly <x> <y> <scale>` | Move the camera at once, or fly it
`/zoom <scale>`, `/fit` | Zoom, show the whole level
`/layer <index>`, `/hide <index>`, `/layers` | Edit a layer, hide it, list the layers in the log
`/paint <layer> <x> <y> <[source:]tile> [width] [height]` | Paint tiles of the default tile set, or of another source as `/addtiles` numbers them
`/undo`, `/redo`, `/action <id>` | Undo, redo, run an action of the menus
`/click <x> <y>`, `/rclick <x> <y>`, `/drag <x1> <y1> <x2> <y2>`, `/hover <x> <y>`, `/wheel <x> <y> <amount>` | Use the pointer
`/renderer <type>` | Changes the renderer of the current layer, see @ref Tiles::LayerRendererType
`/addtiles <source> <image.png> [all] [dither]` | Adds the tiles of an image to a tile set of the level, as <b>Add Tiles from Image</b> does --- with `all`, also those it has already; the source is a number as for `/applytiles`, `level` for the tiles of this level, or `new:<name>` for a new tile set
`/fxtiles <source> <kind> <width> <height>` | Generates effect tiles into the same places as `/addtiles` and makes them the brush; the kind is a number of @ref Editor::FxTileKind
`/newtileset <name> <tiles> [extra\|default\|save]` | Makes a tile set of empty tiles, as <b>New Tile Set</b> does
`/tileoverride <tile ID> <index>` | Fills a tile with one entry of the palette and makes it solid, only in this level
`/applytiles <source> [new name\|-] [palette]` | Saves the tiles of a tile set changed in this level into it, or into a new tile set, see <b>Save Tile Changes into Tile Set</b>; the source is 0 for the default tile set, 1 and on for the extra ones; with `palette`, the palette of the level too
`/palette <entry> <rrggbb>` | Sets an entry of the palette of the level, it has its own then
`/exporttileset <image.png>` | Saves the default tile set and its masks as images, as <b>Export Tile Set Image</b> does
`/importtileset <image.png> <name> [extra\|default\|save] [own\|true\|match\|reduce] [dither]` | Makes a new tile set of an image, as <b>New Tile Set from Image</b> does --- with its colors kept, unless they're said
`/pngtest <directory>` | Reads every PNG image of a directory, compares it with the pixels of an `.rgba` file of the same name if there is one, writes it into `out/` and reads it back
`/key <name>` | Press a key: `enter`, `escape`, `delete`, `f5`, `left`, `right`, `up`, `down`, `space`, `ctrl`, `shift` or `alt`
`/save <path>`, `/export <path>`, `/exportplayonly <path>`, `/autosave` | Write the level, exported only to be played, or its recovery copy
`/texts`, `/findevents <key>` | Log the texts of the level, with their control characters, or where at most 20 events of a kind are
`/keydown <name>`, `/keyup <name>` | Hold a key down, release it --- the game reads held keys once per frame
`/placeevent <key> <x> <y>` | Places an event of the catalog with its default parameters on a tile, as one step
`/placeoffgrid <key> <x> <y> [delay]` | Places an off-grid event of the catalog with its default parameters at a position in pixels, as one step; with a delay in seconds, as a generator of it
`/brush <key>`, `/selectevent <x> <y> [add]`, `/events` | Choose an event to place by its key in the catalog, select the event of a tile or add it to the selection, list the selected events and the brush in the log
`/animframe <index> <frames>` | Change the frame time of an animated tile
`/gamepad <x> <y>` | Act as if a gamepad was used, with its cursor at a position
`/playring <progress>` | Hold the transition into the level being played at a point between 0 and 1
`/exitlevel` | Leave the level being played by its exit
`/wait <frames>`, `/quit` | Wait before the rest of the commands, quit the application

@code{.sh}
Jazz2 /edit prince/01_castle1 /console "/jump 320 320 2" /console "/paint 2 8 8 5 6 4" /console "/wait 30" /console "/quit"
@endcode

*/

}
